  "id": "DrugBank_DB00956 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocodone, a opioid analgesic, is implicated in the pathogenesis of the common cold through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Hydrocodone may be associated with nasopharyngitis, a condition that can lead to the common cold. This relationship is supported by multiple indirect pathways, where Hydrocodone is linked to nasopharyngitis, which in turn is associated with other conditions that contribute to the common cold. Specifically, Hydrocodone's involvement in nasopharyngitis is mediated through the induction of guaiacol and bromodiphenhydramine, both of which are linked to the common cold. These findings suggest that Hydrocodone may exert a modulatory effect on the nasopharyngeal immune response, potentially influencing the development of the common cold. The presence of a consistent and high-confidence score across multiple paths indicates a plausible mechanistic link, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct role of Hydrocodone in the pathogenesis of the common cold.",
